ressures and how they  can affect a public health agency what I  learned in the course of this reporting  through these three very helpful men was  a tale about an administration in  Washington that was real really more  concerned about tamping down public  hysteria than it was about getting out  real facts and real medical information  to the public there were justifiable  reasons for this perhaps because if you  recall the anthrax letters surfaced  right after 911 the most horrendous  terrorist attack in our history and so  the administration and some of the large  federal agencies really didn't want  things to get any worse and they  certainly didn't want the public to be  hysterical about going to the mailbox  and opening mail and finding something  deadly inside it so there was an  elaborate campaign to spin and to  minimize the consequences in danger of  these attacks  but the problem was this was taking  place at the same time that quick  thinking and helpful public information  could have really helped the situation  our public health system during these  attacks was fractured and poorly  coordinated doctors had to go back to  their medical textbooks to even figure  out what anthrax was in some cases  patients would stumble into the hospital  and they would find a doctor who was  alert enough to remember oh yeah I  remember that there was this bizarre  bacteria that caused the lungs to widen  but in many cases the doctors didn't  remember this they didn't they they had  no reason to have that information at  their fingertips and patients went  undiagnosed the communication between  the local agencies and the federal  agencies was very poor and for good  reason there had not been enough money  invested in such basic things as  telephone lines and fax machines and  other forms of communication the CDC it  turned out had operated under an  abundance of caution rather than giving  the quick response and sound medical  advice that was called for everyone at  the 